1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,375
Case PORTFLOW.

2
00:00:01,375 --> 00:00:04,888
PortFlow Container Terminal: Yard and Gate Flow.

3
00:00:04,888 --> 00:00:12,134
In the next two minutes: the situation, what makes it hard, and what the judges are really testing.

4
00:00:13,130 --> 00:00:21,336
PortFlow is a Canadian container terminal linking shipping lines, trucking, rail and inland customers.

5
00:00:21,336 --> 00:00:34,449
Today the yard holds 21,120 TEU against 24,000 TEU effective capacity (88%), containers dwell 6 days, and trucks take 72 minutes per visit (40 queuing, 32 inside).

6
00:00:34,449 --> 00:00:37,264
Volume will grow 20% in five years.

7
00:00:37,264 --> 00:00:43,861
The terminal spends $6M a year on overtime and $8M on delay and service penalties.

8
00:00:43,861 --> 00:00:47,642
Release and schedule information is not shared…

9
00:00:48,640 --> 00:00:54,291
Year-5 yard utilization if nothing changes is 105.60 % of capacity.

10
00:00:54,291 --> 00:01:03,232
The target is a 19.5 percent improvement, within 12 months and a capital ceiling of 10.00 million dollars.

11
00:01:04,230 --> 00:01:06,261
And there are hard limits.

12
00:01:06,261 --> 00:01:12,041
Upfront investment at most $10M CAD; it does not need to be spent in full.

13
00:01:12,041 --> 00:01:19,227
Year-5 objectives: average yard utilization <= 85% and average truck turn time < 45 minutes.

14
00:01:19,227 --> 00:01:24,616
Vessel and truck operations cannot stop while changes are introduced.

15
00:01:24,616 --> 00:01:30,318
Customs holds and missing documentation cannot be overridden by PortFlow.

16
00:01:31,320 --> 00:01:44,664
Which package, within the $10M upfront budget, attacks PortFlow's core issue so that at +20% volume the yard runs at or below 85% utilization and trucks turn in under 45 minutes by year five?

17
00:01:45,660 --> 00:02:02,000
Three alternatives are on the table: option A, Gate quick win: truck appointments and release portal; option B, Integrated flow package: shared visibility, appointments, AI-assisted yard planning; option C, Yard expansion on the 15-acre parcel with two added RTGs.

18
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:05,364
Video two shows how to compare them.

19
00:02:06,360 --> 00:02:09,951
This is a flow problem that looks like a space problem.

20
00:02:09,951 --> 00:02:25,358
Judges test whether you separate causes from symptoms with the case data (Little's Law on dwell), resist the obvious expansion, and still cover finance, operations and safety, supply chain, change management and AI within a $10M budget.

21
00:02:25,358 --> 00:02:29,928
The scoring rewards diagnosis, rigour and a defensible recommendation.
